Q: Hexagonal Grids, how do you find which hexagon a point is in? A: <p>I'm little late to the party, but I've found a simple and mathematically elegant solution to this problem, I haven't seen posted yet. It may be a little performance heavy, because It's doing a few divisions.</p>
<p>This works best with axial/cube coordinates, a hexagonal shaped grid and normalized data also makes it easier.</p>
<p>To visualize what I am doing, it helps to first look at the same thing in a square grid.</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/ERwQAuZP.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/ERwQAuZP.png" alt="square-grid-example-vector" /></a></p>
<p>Here I want to convert the <code>floatVector P(1.25, 1.75)</code> to it's corresponding grid coordinate. To do that, I scale the vector by <code>1/tileSize</code> and round the values. Note that when using normalized coordinates, like here, the <code>tileSize = 1</code>, so I save on that division.
On a square grid the axis vectors would look like this:<code>X(1, 0)</code> and <code>Y(0, 1)</code>. Based on that increments to x and y can be made. Because they each only manipulate one component, they can just be merged into a single vector.</p>
<p>In math terms, I solve this:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/0S2uODCY.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/0S2uODCY.png" alt="square-grid-math" /></a></p>
<p>for x and y (grid coordinates) and round them after.</p>
<p>Now I just use this same method on my two hexagonal axis <code>l</code> and <code>r</code>. They move up to the left and right respectively. Using a hexagonal shaped grid has the advantage, that the distance from the center is the same everywhere, making bounds checking easy.</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/LhxoX6Rd.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/LhxoX6Rd.png" alt="hex-grid" /></a></p>
<p>Moving forward I will assume the coordinates normalized to the radius of a single hexagon.</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/JfQKM1v2.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/JfQKM1v2.png" alt="hex-axis" /></a></p>
<p>Here the axis vectors look a little different: <code>L(-cos(alpha), sin(alpha)</code> <code>R(cos(alpha), sin(alpha))</code>. For the grid I chose <code>alpha = 2PI/6</code>. If your hexagons rotate, you will need to calculate <code>cos()</code> and <code>sin()</code> each time, impacting your performance heavily, but there is not really a way around that. I just the macros <code>COS_HEX_ANGLE</code> and <code>SIN_HEX_ANGLE</code>.</p>
<p>Combining all this these parts, I get this equation.</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/D6ATG14E.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/D6ATG14E.png" alt="hex-axis-math" /></a></p>
<p>Here <code>'s'</code> and <code>'c'</code>stand for <code>SIN_HEX_ANGLE</code> and <code>COS_HEX_ANGLE</code>.</p>
<p>I would be mad, if I solved for <code>l</code> and <code>r</code> myself, so here is some <a href="https://www.wolframalpha.com/input?i2d=true&amp;i=solve%5C%2840%29l*%7B%7B-c%7D%2C%7Bs%7D%7D%2Br*%7B%7Bc%7D%2C%7Bs%7D%7D%3D%7B%7Bx%7D%2C%7By%7D%7D%5C%2841%29+for%5C%2840%29l%5C%2844%29+r%5C%2841%29%5C%2844%29" rel="nofollow noreferrer">wolfram alpha</a> magic:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/bZaWFRXU.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/bZaWFRXU.png" alt="l,r-math" /></a></p>
<p>And that's basically it. I also divide my values by the <code>tileDiameter</code>, which is just 2, because I use normalized coordinates. I check if that value is inside my grid and then round to my coordinate. All of that amounts to 8 lines of c code. I tested this and it works.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>bool pointGridCollision(fVector2 point, int8_t* lReturn, int8_t* rReturn){
    float l = 0.25f*((point.y/SIN_HEX_ANGLE) - (point.x/COS_HEX_ANGLE));
    float r = (COS_HEX_ANGLE*point.y + SIN_HEX_ANGLE*point.x)/(4*SIN_HEX_ANGLE*COS_HEX_ANGLE);

    if(abs(lroundf(l)) &lt;= GRID_RADIUS &amp;&amp; abs(lroundf(r)) &lt;= GRID_RADIUS){
        *lReturn = (int8_t)lroundf(l);
        *rReturn = (int8_t)lroundf(r);

        return true;
    }else return false;
}
</code></pre>
<p>It returns <code>true</code>, if the coordinate is inside the grid and <code>false</code> if not.</p>
<p>I have the <code>gridRadius</code> set in a macro, because it doesn't change for me, but you could also pass it as a parameter, if it suits you.<code>lroundf()</code> returns a rounded float as an integer. <code>fVector2</code> is just a 2D float vector. You might want to adjust the datatype of <code>lReturn</code> and <code>rReturn</code> to a something, that can store more bytes, if your <code>gridRadius &gt; 128</code>.</p>
